```

### Best Practices
- Always validate input data (use Zod)
- Implement proper error handling
- Return consistent response formats
- Use appropriate HTTP status codes
- Implement rate limiting for public endpoints
- Add CORS headers when needed
- Log errors for debugging

## Server Actions

### Server Action Pattern
```typescript
// app/actions/user-actions.ts
'use server';

import { z } from 'zod';
import { revalidatePath } from 'next/cache';

const createUserSchema = z.object({
  name: z.string().min(1),
  email: z.string().email(),
});

export async function createUser(formData: FormData) {
  const rawData = {
    name: formData.get('name'),
    email: formData.get('email'),
  };

  const validatedData = createUserSchema.parse(rawData);
  
  // Database operation
  const user = await db.user.create({ data: validatedData });
  
  revalidatePath('/users');
  return { success: true, user };
}
```

### Server Action Best Practices
- Always use `'use server'` directive
- Validate all inputs with Zod
- Handle errors gracefully
- Use revalidatePath/revalidateTag for cache invalidation
- Never expose sensitive data
- Implement proper authorization checks
- Return consistent response formats

## Middleware

### Middleware Pattern
```typescript
// middleware.ts
import { NextResponse } from 'next/server';
import type { NextRequest } from 'next/server';

export function middleware(request: NextRequest) {
  // Authentication check
  const token = request.cookies.get('auth-token');
  
  if (!token && request.nextUrl.pathname.startsWith('/dashboard')) {
    return NextResponse.redirect(new URL('/login', request.url));
  }
  
  return NextResponse.next();
}

export const config = {
  matcher: ['/dashboard/:path*', '/api/protected/:path*'],
};
```

### Middleware Use Cases
- Authentication and authorization
- Request logging
- Rate limiting
- A/B testing
- Geolocation-based routing
- Header manipulation

## Request Validation

### Zod Schema Pattern
```typescript
import { z } from 'zod';

const apiRequestSchema = z.object({
  name: z.string().min(1).max(100),
  email: z.string().email(),
  age: z.number().int().positive().optional(),
});

export async function POST(request: NextRequest) {
  try {
    const body = await request.json();
    const validatedData = apiRequestSchema.parse(body);
    // Process validated data
  } catch (error) {
    if (error instanceof z.ZodError) {
      return NextResponse.json(
        { error: 'Validation failed', details: error.errors },
        { status: 400 }
      );
    }
    throw error;
  }
}
```

## Error Handling

### Standardized Error Response
```typescript
interface ApiError {
  error: string;
  code?: string;
  details?: unknown;
}

export function createErrorResponse(
  error: unknown,
  status: number = 500
): NextResponse<ApiError> {
  const message = error instanceof Error ? error.message : 'Unknown error';
  return NextResponse.json(
    { error: message },
    { status }
  );
}
```

## Rate Limiting

- Implement rate limiting for public APIs
- Use middleware or API route level rate limiting
- Return appropriate 429 status codes
- Include rate limit headers in responses

## Security Best Practices

- Never trust client input - always validate
- Sanitize user inputs
- Use parameterized queries (Prisma handles this)
- Implement CSRF protection for mutations
- Validate file uploads (type, size)
- Use environment variables for secrets
- Implement proper CORS policies

## Database Integration

- Use Prisma for type-safe database access
- Implement proper transaction handling
- Use connection pooling
- Handle database errors gracefully
- Implement retry logic for transient errors

## External API Integration

- Use proper error handling and retries
- Implement timeout mechanisms
- Cache responses when appropriate
- Handle rate limits from external APIs
- Use environment variables for API keys

## Response Formats

### Success Response
```typescript
{
  success: true,
  data: { ... },
  message?: string
}
```

### Error Response
```typescript
{
  success: false,
  error: "Error message",
  code?: string,
  details?: unknown
}
```

